    "question": {
      ":question/id": "28a1ba26-1046-5811-bb62-936ac0cbc33b",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $-x+y=4$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $-x+y=4$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+y &= 4 \\\\\ny &= x+4 \\\\\ny &= x+4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $1$.\n\nOnly $y=x+3$ has slope $1$.\n\nThe line $y=x+3$ has slope $1$ and $y$-intercept $3$, different from $4$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\ny=x+3\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
