    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7842289a-e710-5b96-882b-31c0735d2ce5",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $6x-3y=3$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $6x-3y=3$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x-3y &= 3 \\\\\n-3y &= -6x+3 \\\\\ny &= 2x-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $2$.\n\nOnly $y=2x-2$ has slope $2$.\n\nThe line $y=2x-2$ has slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $-2$, different from $-1$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\ny=2x-2\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
