    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8075e4dc-b17a-5619-93f8-b386fd446b8a",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $9x+3y=-6$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For two distinct nonvertical lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of $9x+3y=-6$, convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x+3y &= -6 \\\\\n3y &= -9x-6 \\\\\ny &= -3x-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope is the coefficient of $x$, so the required slope is $-3$.\n\nOnly $y=-3x+5$ has slope $-3$.\n\nThe line $y=-3x+5$ has slope $-3$ and $y$-intercept $5$, different from $-2$. Therefore, it is a distinct parallel line.\n\n$$\ny=-3x+5\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
